(* Go+ v0.20.0 grammar delta: match guards, in both surfaces.

   The complete base grammar is Go plus the declarations and expressions
   in grammar-v0.19.0.ebnf. One production changes (gap program C2a,
   spec/gap-program-design.md; the reserved guard production from the
   goml design flips on).
*)

CaseClause  = "case" [ identifier ":=" ] Pattern { "," Pattern }
              [ "if" Expression ] ":" StatementList .        (* .gp *)
            (* goml: Clauses = { "|" PatternList [ "if" Expr ] "=>" Expr } *)

(*
  ## A guard runs its arm only when it holds

      case Cons(h, t) if h > n:      -- .gp
      | Cons h t if h > n =>          -- goml

  A false guard falls through to the next arm, in order. The guard is
  evaluated after the pattern's bindings, so it sees the binders — and
  a binder referenced only by the guard still binds.

  ## Guards contribute nothing to exhaustiveness

  This is the decided C2a policy: a guard may be false, so a guarded
  arm covers no variant. Exhaustiveness still demands unguarded
  coverage (an unguarded arm or a 'case _:'), and a guarded arm's own
  reachability is judged by its pattern against the unguarded arms
  above it. Decider-checked guard coverage (complement pairs proving a
  variant covered) is deliberately later — it lands with views in C3,
  where hypothesis-aware coverage has two consumers.

  ## Lowering

  A guarded match lowers through the goto chain (a type switch cannot
  fall through on a failed check); the guard is one more check between
  the bindings and the body. Refusals: a wildcard cannot be guarded (it
  would leave the match open), a multi-pattern arm cannot be guarded
  (split the arm), an impossible arm cannot be guarded (it asserts the
  pattern is ruled out for every value).

  Literal patterns — the other half of the reserved production — are
  C2b, with decider-driven interval coverage.
*)
